# ais

`ais` is a Go CLI for the watchdog gRPC service. It connects to the Unix
socket at `/tmp/artisan_watchdog.sock` by default.

The production socket is mode `0600` and watchdog also checks Unix peer
credentials, so commands must run as root (normally with `sudo`).

## Build

Requirements:

- Go 1.24 or newer
- `protoc`
- `protoc-gen-go` and `protoc-gen-go-grpc`

```bash
make proto  # regenerate Go protobuf and gRPC code
make build  # build ./ais
make        # run both steps
```

The canonical service contract is `../../proto/watchdog.proto`; keep the CLI
copy at `proto/watchdog.proto` byte-identical when changing the API.

## Config setup and editing

Scaffold and edit every application found in the encrypted git credentials:

```bash
sudo ./ais setup
```

Limit setup to one application using its full ID, bare hash, or an unambiguous
hash prefix:

```bash
sudo ./ais setup ais_1a2b3c4d
sudo ./ais setup 1a2b3c4d
sudo ./ais setup 1a2b
```

`setup` creates missing `/opt/artisan/etc/<ais_id>/Config.toml` and
`Overrides.toml` placeholders, then offers to stop the app before opening both
files. Existing files are never replaced during scaffolding.

Edit existing files with:

```bash
sudo ./ais edit ais_1a2b3c4d
sudo ./ais edit 1a2b3c4d config
sudo ./ais edit 1a2b3c4d overrides
```

`edit` does not scaffold. If a requested file is missing, run `ais setup`
first. The editor is selected from `$VISUAL`, then `$EDITOR`, then `vi`.

Before saving, the CLI validates TOML locally and the watchdog validates it
again. The watchdog rejects stale edits using a SHA-256 concurrency token. Each
successful replacement creates a timestamped `.aold` backup and retains the
newest five backups per file.

If an app was running, the CLI stops it before editing and starts it after all
selected files are handled. If editing is interrupted, it asks whether the app
should be restarted so a stopped process is not silently stranded.

## Other commands

```text
list
info
status <application>
start|stop|reload|rebuild <application>
get <application> <field>
set <application> <field> <value>
usage <application> [start] [end]
logs-current <application> [limit]
logs-history <application> [stream] [start] [end] [limit] [cursor]
setup [application]
edit <application> [config|overrides]
```

Use a non-default socket with `--socket` before the command:

```bash
sudo ./ais --socket /path/to/watchdog.sock list
```
